        {
          "info": {
            "name": "Get trace by transaction",
            "type": "http"
          },
          "http": {
            "method": "GET",
            "url": "https://api.sentio.xyz/v1/solidity/:owner/:slug/:chainId/transaction/:txHash/call_trace",
            "params": [
              {
                "name": "owner",
                "value": "",
                "type": "path"
              },
              {
                "name": "slug",
                "value": "",
                "type": "path"
              },
              {
                "name": "chainId",
                "value": "",
                "type": "path"
              },
              {
                "name": "txHash",
                "value": "",
                "type": "path"
              },
              {
                "name": "withInternalCalls",
                "value": "",
                "type": "query",
                "description": "Fetch the decoded trace, which will give you the function info, decoded parameters of both external and internal call trace."
              },
              {
                "name": "disableOptimizer",
                "value": "",
                "type": "query",
                "description": "Disable optimizations to make internal calls more accurate, but gas costs will differ from the actual execution."
              },
              {
                "name": "ignoreGasCost",
                "value": "",
                "type": "query",
                "description": "Only effective when disableOptimizer=true."
              }
            ]
          },
          "docs": "API to get Sentio call trace. It takes `txId.txHash` and `chainSpec.chainId` arguments, where the first is transaction hash, and the second is the numeric ethereum chain ID.\n\nThe results looks very similar to the normal [Ethereum call trace](https://media.githubusercontent.com/media/sentioxyz/docs/HEAD/assets/image%20(2)%20(1)%20(1)%20(1).png). But we have an additional `startIndex` and `startIndex` on each trace entry even for the LOG, representing the execution order in the trace.\n\nThis allows"
        },
